    "id": "train-2089",
    "question": "Sophia ate 1/6 of her pie and she put the rest on the fridge. If the pie left in the fridge weighs 1200 grams, how many grams did Sophia eat?",
    "gold": "240",
    "answer": "If Sophia ate 1/6 of the pie, then 6/6 - 1/6 = 5/6 is left in the fridge.\nLet x be the pie's original weight.\nThe current weight of the pie can be described by 5x/6=1200 grams\nSo, 5x=7200.\nAnd, the original weight is x = <<1440=1440>>1440 grams.\nSo, Sophia ate 1440 grams original - 1200 grams after= <<1440-1200=240>>240 grams of pie.\n#### 240"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how many grams of pie Sophia ate, we start by understanding the relationship between the fraction of the pie she ate and the weight of the pie left in the fridge.\n\n1. Let the total weight of the pie be \\( W \\) grams.\n2. Sophia ate \\( \\frac{1}{6} \\) of the pie, so the weight of the pie she ate is \\( \\frac{1}{6}W \\).\n3. The weight of the pie left in the fridge is given as 1200 grams. This means that the weight of the pie she ate plus the weight of the pie left in the fridge is equal to the total weight of the pie. Therefore, we can write the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{6}W + 1200 = W\n   \\]\n4. To find \\( W \\), we first isolate \\( W \\) on one side of the equation. Subtract \\( \\frac{1}{6}W \\) from both sides:\n   \\[\n   1200 = W - \\frac{1}{6}W\n   \\]\n5. Combine the terms on the right side:\n   \\[\n   1200 = \\frac{6}{6}W - \\frac{1}{6}W = \\frac{5}{6}W\n   \\]\n6. To solve for \\( W \\), multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of \\( \\frac{5}{6} \\), which is \\( \\frac{6}{5} \\):\n   \\[\n   W = 1200 \\times \\frac{6}{5} = 1440\n   \\]\n7. Now that we know the total weight of the pie is 1440 grams, we can find out how much Sophia ate. Since she ate \\( \\frac{1}{6} \\) of the pie, we calculate:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Weight of the pie Sophia ate} = \\frac{1}{6} \\times 1440 = 240\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of grams Sophia ate is \\(\\boxed{240}\\).",
